  "hasDedicatedReadOnlyLevel": false,
  "answerToTheReadOnlyQuestion": "Observed. Field Security has NO distinct 'ReadOnly' access level - its vocabulary is the same four values as Page Access and Actions. **But read-only-ness IS expressible here, as `View`.** Granting `View` on a field lets a user class see the field without editing it, which is read-only in effect. So the capability exists; it simply is not spelled 'ReadOnly'.",
  "whyTheFieldCatalogDisagrees": "Derived. The catalog's `ReadOnly = No` on all 6,158 leaves and Field Security's per-class `View` are measuring DIFFERENT things, which is why the catalog looked wrong. The catalog column is a property of the field DEFINITION (is this field inherently non-editable for everyone), whereas read-only-ness in practice is a per-user-class GRANT. A field can be universally editable by definition and still be read-only for eight of ten user classes. The catalog is not necessarily in error - it answers a question nobody was asking.",
  "caution": "The strings `Is ReadOnly?` and `Read Only?` DO appear in this page, but they are the NAMES OF DATA FIELDS being secured (alongside `Allow UI Edit?`, `Is Inactive?`), not security options. Do not mistake them for an access level.",
  "defaultSelectedClass": "System Administrator (7885) - note this tab defaults to a DIFFERENT user class than Page Access, which defaults to Default Security (7884). Always read the returned <select> to confirm which class a value belongs to.",
  "userClassCount": 10
 },
